# GW230529 AWS Terraform -- shell environment template
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored.
#
# The Makefile sources this file, so it only needs values that are used by
# the AWS CLI / helper scripts. Terraform inputs live in the per-stack
# terraform.tfvars files instead.

# ============================================================
# AWS credentials
# ============================================================
# Named profile from ~/.aws/credentials or ~/.aws/config.
# Using a profile keeps long-lived keys out of the shell environment.
# The profile that assumes the operator role. It needs role_arn, mfa_serial
# and source_profile in ~/.aws/config -- `make output-bootstrap` prints a
# ready-to-paste block.
#
# Terraform cannot prompt for an MFA token, so a session has to be in the
# environment before any target that talks to AWS:
#
#   eval "$(make login)"
#
# That sets AWS_SESSION_TOKEN, and the Makefile then stops exporting this
# variable so the session is not overridden by it.
AWS_PROFILE=gw230529

# Region the foundation stack is deployed into.
# Fixed once and for all: ECR images and S3 objects are region-bound, so
# moving later means re-pushing the 4.06 GB image. Run `make region-scout`
# before deciding.
AWS_REGION=us-west-2

# ============================================================
# Region scouting
# ============================================================
# Candidate regions compared by scripts/region_scout.sh.
SCOUT_REGIONS="us-east-1 us-east-2 us-west-2"

# Instance types to score. The first entry is the production target.
#
# Memory is the binding constraint, not price. The published reference run
# reports 438.5 GB across its 12 nodes at 480 ranks. Fewer ranks duplicate
# fewer ghost zones so a 192 rank run needs less than that, but not
# proportionally less -- 384 GiB is not a comfortable ceiling to plan against.
#
# m7a.48xlarge  AMD Genoa, 192 physical cores (SMT off), 12 DDR5 channels,
#               768 GiB. The only single-node size with real headroom over
#               the reference figure. ~26% dearer per core than c7a.
# c7a.48xlarge  Same cores and channels, 384 GiB, the best price per physical
#               core in us-west-2. Viable only if Phase 5 measures the working
#               set well below 384 GiB.
# r7a.48xlarge  Same cores again, 1536 GiB, ~42% dearer per core than c7a.
#               Scored as the escape hatch if 768 GiB also proves short.
#
# c7i.48xlarge was dropped after the 2026-08-20 price check: its 192 vCPUs are
# 96 physical Sapphire Rapids cores plus hyperthreading on 8 memory channels,
# so at 3.07 USD/h it costs 0.0320 USD per physical core-hour against c7a's
# 0.0155 -- 2.06x for a bandwidth-bound evolution. The near-identical hourly
# price is what makes it a trap, and no scouting run is going to change that.
#
# Smaller sizes are not an option at full resolution: c7a.24xlarge has 192 GiB,
# and it also costs 31% more per physical core than the 48xlarge.
SCOUT_INSTANCE_TYPES="m7a.48xlarge c7a.48xlarge r7a.48xlarge"

# Target capacity in vCPUs used for the spot placement score query.
SCOUT_TARGET_VCPUS=192

# ============================================================
# Capacity ladder (scripts/launch_with_ladder.sh, used by make run)
# ============================================================
# The pools a launch may try, in order, as zone:instance-type. Unset means
# one attempt with whatever terraform.tfvars says, which is what make run did
# before the ladder existed.
#
# A rung crosses BOTH dimensions, and that is the lesson of 2026-08-27 rather
# than a generalisation. The crunch that evening was specific to the c7a
# pools: five consecutive c7a nodes in us-west-2a were reaped inside half an
# hour each and banked nothing, while an m7a request in the same zone was
# filled on the first sixty second poll. c7a and m7a 48xlarge are the same
# Genoa silicon with the same twelve memory channels, so for this evolution
# the only thing c7a buys is the lower price -- and a search that refuses to
# leave the c7a row optimises 0.7 USD/h while burning 3 USD/h recomputing
# work it keeps losing.
#
# Ordered by measured spot price (2026-08-19, us-west-2, 48xlarge):
#   c7a 2d 2.97   c7a 2a 3.05   c7a 2c 3.32   then m7a at about 3.75
#
# Every zone named here needs a subnet from the foundation stack; the script
# checks that before it attempts anything. r7a is left out: 1536 GiB has no
# use here, since the working set measured 125 GB.
# LAUNCH_LADDER="us-west-2d:c7a.48xlarge us-west-2a:c7a.48xlarge us-west-2c:c7a.48xlarge us-west-2d:m7a.48xlarge us-west-2a:m7a.48xlarge us-west-2c:m7a.48xlarge"

# How hard each rung is tried before moving on.
#
# InsufficientInstanceCapacity is an EC2 server error, so the SDK retries it
# like any 500 -- at the provider default of 25 attempts, one rung would hold
# the ladder for close to an hour. Four attempts is a couple of minutes, and
# the ladder cycles: sampling six pools ten times finds one that frees up
# sooner than waiting on the first. Nothing is created or billed by an
# attempt that fails this way.
# LADDER_RETRIES=4
# LADDER_PASSES=3
# LADDER_PASS_SLEEP=300

# ============================================================
# Terraform plugin cache
# ============================================================
# Shared provider download directory. The AWS provider is around 840 MB and
# all three stacks use it, so without a shared cache the checkout triples.
# The Makefile sets this automatically; override it here if the home
# directory is on slow storage.
# TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR=${HOME}/.terraform.d/plugin-cache
